\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two mathematically distinct limits. (a) The declared past-cone road law $A_{\rm  los}(z)=\pi ^{-1}[1-(1+z)^{-3}]$ approaches $1/\pi $ as redshift increases for a fixed observer. (b) With radiation neglected, $\Omega _m=1/\pi $ and $\Omega _\Lambda =1-1/\pi $, the adopted flat matter--constant-vacuum adapter gives $H_{\rm  native}(a)/H_0=\sqrt  {\Omega _m a^{-3}+\Omega _\Lambda }$ and tends to $\sqrt  {\Omega _\Lambda }$. The lower dotted reference, $\Omega _\Lambda ^{3/2}$, is a conditional distance-fit coefficient, not the native fractional-expansion limit; it requires the additional future observing-family and calibration assumptions in the text. Past-cone saturation does not derive a future road limit. These curves are analytic illustrations of declared premises, not observational data or an independently derived SAM future.}}{4}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:c12:limits}{{1}{4}{Two mathematically distinct limits. (a) The declared past-cone road law $A_{\rm los}(z)=\pi ^{-1}[1-(1+z)^{-3}]$ approaches $1/\pi $ as redshift increases for a fixed observer. (b) With radiation neglected, $\Omega _m=1/\pi $ and $\Omega _\Lambda =1-1/\pi $, the adopted flat matter--constant-vacuum adapter gives $H_{\rm native}(a)/H_0=\sqrt {\Omega _m a^{-3}+\Omega _\Lambda }$ and tends to $\sqrt {\Omega _\Lambda }$. The lower dotted reference, $\Omega _\Lambda ^{3/2}$, is a conditional distance-fit coefficient, not the native fractional-expansion limit; it requires the additional future observing-family and calibration assumptions in the text. Past-cone saturation does not derive a future road limit. These curves are analytic illustrations of declared premises, not observational data or an independently derived SAM future}{figure.caption.26}{}}
